  "epilogue": [
    "By midnight, the police had taken over the Rookery building and the Vesper announcement was cancelled. The station’s stream played a silent holding loop until dawn, while the people who had spent years filling the archive boxes waited in the corridor outside.",
    "Clare Vale refused the first offer from Vesper Media. The sale eventually went through the following spring, but the archive stayed in Brighton, under a trust that paid its royalties to the people who had made the programmes. Tom’s late-night show returned with a new producer and a strict rule: no one was allowed to call a recording live.",
    "Ben kept the old automation rack in a glass case. He labelled the button that had fooled everyone for one night: AUTO-RESCUE. Underneath, in Marcus’s handwriting, someone added a second label. LISTEN CLOSELY.",
    "Rookery’s archive still has a gap on one shelf where the Clearwater file used to be. The photocopy is kept in a locked drawer now, beside the master keys and the brass reel weight. Nobody at the company calls the empty space a memorial. They call it a warning."
  ]
